# SignalDeck daemon config — copy to .env and fill. .env is gitignored.
# LLM provider key (NVIDIA is OpenAI-compatible at integrate.api.nvidia.com/v1).
# SIGNALDECK_NVIDIA_KEY=nvapi-...
# Optional: a POOL of keys for round-robin + failover (rotates on 429/5xx/bad-key).
# Add more free NVIDIA keys here to raise throughput; overrides the single key.
# SIGNALDECK_NVIDIA_KEYS=nvapi-key1,nvapi-key2,nvapi-key3
# Model tiers (all optional — sensible NVIDIA-free-tier defaults are built in):
#   default  = the workhorse (qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b — MoE, strong + ~4s)
#   deep     = on-demand reasoning for analyst/debate/scenario (nemotron-super-49b, ~30s)
#   fast     = highest-frequency, lowest-stakes calls e.g. sentiment (llama-3.1-8b)
# SIGNALDECK_LLM_MODEL=qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b
# SIGNALDECK_LLM_MODEL_DEEP=nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1.5
# SIGNALDECK_LLM_MODEL_FAST=meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct
# Hard daily cap on total LLM calls (spend guard; pooled across keys). Default 2000.
# SIGNALDECK_LLM_DAILY_CAP=2000
# Optional bearer token — set this to require auth on EVERY API request
# (needed before exposing the daemon beyond localhost). SERVER-ONLY SECRET.
# To let the web app reach a token-protected daemon, put the SAME value in
# web/.env.local as SIGNALDECK_API_TOKEN (see web/.env.example) — the Next.js
# /api/* proxy route reads it server-side and it never reaches the browser.
# NEVER name it NEXT_PUBLIC_*: Next.js substitutes every NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable
# into the client JS bundle at BUILD time, so the literal value ships to every
# visitor and is readable in devtools/view-source. There is no runtime check
# that catches this — the build simply inlines it. This token maps to the
# ADMIN user in the daemon's resolveUser(), so publishing it hands any visitor
# full admin. Browsers authenticate with the HttpOnly session cookie instead;
# the bearer exists for non-browser clients that call the daemon directly.
# SIGNALDECK_API_TOKEN=
# Comma-separated CORS-allowed web origins (defaults cover localhost:8323/3000).
# SIGNALDECK_WEB_ORIGINS=http://localhost:8323
# Comma-separated allowed Host headers (blocks DNS rebinding).
# SIGNALDECK_ALLOWED_HOSTS=127.0.0.1:8322,localhost:8322
# ── Stage 2 free data (both optional; the daemon works with NONE of these) ──
# FRED macro (VIX/yields/spread/fed-funds) works with NO key via the keyless
# CSV endpoint. Set this ONLY to use FRED's JSON API instead. Get a free key at
# https://fredaccount.stlouisfed.org/apikeys
# SIGNALDECK_FRED_KEY=
# SEC EDGAR needs NO key, but SEC's fair-access policy (and its WAF) REQUIRES
# a declarative User-Agent with a real, deliverable contact — anonymous UAs get
# 403'd fleet-wide (fundamentals, filings, Form 4, 13F). Set ONE of these in
# daemon/.env:
# Contact email → UA becomes "SignalDeck/0.1 (<email>)". RECOMMENDED.
# SIGNALDECK_CONTACT_EMAIL=you@example.com
# Full UA override (verbatim), e.g. "Sample Company Name admin@sample.com".
# SIGNALDECK_EDGAR_UA=
# ── Stage 3 remote alert delivery (ALL optional; none set = macOS-only) ──
# Batched alert + watchdog notifications fan out to EVERY configured transport
# below. Contract: 5s timeout + 1 retry per delivery; failures become
# dq_events (kind notify_failed, secrets redacted) and NEVER block the fleet.
# Check GET /api/notify-status (or the note on /signals/alerts) to see what's
# live. NOTHING SET = alerts reach nobody but a local desktop toast whose
# delivery is not tracked (and which draws nothing at all from a service
# session) — set at least one transport below.
# Discord: server → channel → Edit Channel → Integrations → Webhooks → copy URL.
# SIGNALDECK_DISCORD_WEBHOOK=
# Telegram: create a bot via @BotFather (gives the token), then message the
# bot once and read your chat id from
# https://api.telegram.org/bot<TOKEN>/getUpdates (chat.id). BOTH required.
# SIGNALDECK_TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
# SIGNALDECK_TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=
# Generic webhook: POSTs JSON {"title","body","kind","ts"} to this URL
# (point it at ntfy/your own receiver). NOT Slack-compatible — Slack
# answers that shape with 400 invalid_payload; use the Slack var below.
# SIGNALDECK_WEBHOOK_URL=
# Slack: Slack app → Incoming Webhooks → Add New Webhook to Workspace.
# POSTs {"text"} to the URL, which is itself the credential (redacted
# from every log line, dq detail and status string).
# SIGNALDECK_SLACK_WEBHOOK=
# Email (SMTP). HOST + FROM + TO are required; the rest are optional.
# PORT defaults to 587 (STARTTLS); 465 dials implicit TLS. USER/PASS
# enable AUTH — and the daemon REFUSES to send them over an unencrypted
# connection, so a server that offers no STARTTLS fails the delivery
# instead of leaking the password. ONE attempt, 15s, no retry.
# SIGNALDECK_SMTP_HOST=
# SIGNALDECK_SMTP_PORT=
# SIGNALDECK_SMTP_USER=
# SIGNALDECK_SMTP_PASS=
# SIGNALDECK_SMTP_FROM=
# SIGNALDECK_SMTP_TO=
#
# VERIFY IT AFTER SETTING ONE. Restart the daemon, then:
#   curl -X POST -H 'X-Signaldeck: 1' http://127.0.0.1:8322/api/notify/test
# It sends a real message through every configured transport and reports each
# one's status AFTER the attempt. This matters because delivery is deliberately
# best-effort: a mistyped token does not raise an error, it fails quietly into a
# dq event, so without this call you would not learn about it until the first
# alert you missed.


# ── Alert delivery (documented 2026-08-07 pre-ship audit) ──────────────────
# ALL FIVE TRANSPORTS ARE IMPLEMENTED AND NONE IS CONFIGURED. Until one is set,
# logs/backup-offline.log repeats, correctly, every night:
#
#   NOTIFY: SignalDeck: alerts are SILENT beyond this machine — No
#   Discord/Telegram/webhook transport configured — daemon alerts stay local
#   and go unseen when nobody is at the keyboard.
#
# That is the whole gap: the code is done, the credential is not. Set ONE of
# these, restart the daemon, then confirm delivery end to end with
#   curl -X POST -H "X-Signaldeck: 1" -H "Authorization: Bearer $SIGNALDECK_API_TOKEN" \
#        http://127.0.0.1:8322/api/notify/test
# A transport that has never delivered a test message is not configured, it is
# merely spelled.
#
# Easiest, no account beyond one you already have — Discord server settings ->
# Integrations -> Webhooks -> New Webhook -> Copy URL:
# SIGNALDECK_DISCORD_WEBHOOK=https://discord.com/api/webhooks/…
#
# Telegram: message @BotFather -> /newbot -> copy the token; then message your
# new bot once and read the chat id from
# https://api.telegram.org/bot<TOKEN>/getUpdates
# SIGNALDECK_TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=123456:AA…
# SIGNALDECK_TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=123456789
#
# Slack: Incoming Webhooks app -> Add to Workspace -> copy URL.
# SIGNALDECK_SLACK_WEBHOOK=https://hooks.slack.com/services/…
#
# Any HTTP endpoint (Pushover/ntfy/your own): receives a JSON POST.
# SIGNALDECK_WEBHOOK_URL=https://ntfy.sh/your-private-topic
#
# Email. SMTP_PASS is an APP PASSWORD, never your account password.
# SIGNALDECK_SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
# SIGNALDECK_SMTP_PORT=587
# SIGNALDECK_SMTP_USER=you@example.com
# SIGNALDECK_SMTP_PASS=app-password-not-your-real-one
# SIGNALDECK_SMTP_FROM=you@example.com
# SIGNALDECK_SMTP_TO=you@example.com

# ── Security and operations (documented 2026-08-07 pre-ship audit) ─────────
# 120 SIGNALDECK_* variables exist in the code; ~15 were documented, and the
# undocumented set included every switch that decides whether this daemon is
# exposed. That is the gap this section closes. Defaults quoted from
# daemon/internal/config/config.go.

# ── Paths ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Root containing signaldeck/. Set = used verbatim and the tree is NOT probed;
# this is the ONLY thing that isolates a process from the checkout it runs in.
# Setting it to an empty string panics on purpose. Default: probe, then
# $HOME/claude code.
# SIGNALDECK_ROOT=C:\Users\you\Desktop\claude code
# Database file. Default: <root>/signaldeck/data/signaldeck.db
# SIGNALDECK_DB=C:\path\to\signaldeck.db

# ── HTTP security ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Bind address. Default "127.0.0.1:8322". A bare ":8322" binds EVERY interface
# and is treated as public — which also flips the two open-by-default flags
# below to closed. That is intended, not a bug.
# SIGNALDECK_HTTP=127.0.0.1:8322
#
# Host-header allowlist (blocks DNS rebinding). Default
# "127.0.0.1:8322,localhost:8322". EMPTY DENIES EVERYTHING.
# READ THIS BEFORE ADDING A PUBLIC HOSTNAME: a non-loopback entry here is now
# treated as proof the daemon is published, which closes PUBLIC_READS and
# OPEN_SIGNUP by default. Serving a host you cannot reach from loopback IS
# publication. This replaced a macOS-only LaunchAgent path check that silently
# became a no-op on Windows and left the daemon open.
# SIGNALDECK_ALLOWED_HOSTS=127.0.0.1:8322,localhost:8322,your-tunnel.ngrok-free.dev
#
# CORS origin allowlist for the browser app. Default
# "http://localhost:8323,http://127.0.0.1:8323,http://localhost:3000,http://127.0.0.1:3000".
# No wildcard is ever echoed. A non-GET from an unlisted origin is refused.
# SIGNALDECK_WEB_ORIGINS=http://localhost:8323
#
# Bearer token for NON-BROWSER callers (ops scripts, the anchor publisher).
# Maps to the ADMIN user, so treat it as an admin password. Never send it from
# the browser proxy — doing so hands admin to anonymous visitors. Required once
# PUBLIC_READS is closed, or ops/signaldeck-ctl.sh gets 401.
# SIGNALDECK_API_TOKEN=generate-with-openssl-rand-hex-32
#
# Unauthenticated reads. Default: TRUE only when loopback-bound AND no
# publication signal (see ALLOWED_HOSTS). Setting this true on a reachable
# deployment publishes every read endpoint to anyone who finds the URL.
# SIGNALDECK_PUBLIC_READS=false
#
# Open registration. Same default rule. True on a reachable deployment lets a
# stranger create an account and spend your LLM budget — and if the users
# table is EMPTY, the first registrant becomes ADMIN.
# SIGNALDECK_OPEN_SIGNUP=false
#
# Honor X-Forwarded-For / X-Forwarded-Proto. Default false.
# ONLY set this true when the daemon is unreachable except through a proxy you
# control. Both headers are client-spoofable, so trusting them on a directly
# reachable bind lets an attacker forge the rate-limit key and the "this was
# HTTPS" signal that marks the session cookie Secure.
# SIGNALDECK_TRUST_PROXY=false
#
# Override the publication signal. "1"/"true" forces the open defaults CLOSED —
# use it for a tunnel the allowlist does not name. Overrides everything above.
# SIGNALDECK_ASSUME_TUNNEL=true
#
# TradingView webhook shared secret. EMPTY DISABLES the endpoint (fails
# closed). Never put it in a URL: the ?secret= form is rejected outright
# because tunnels, proxies and access logs all record URLs.
# SIGNALDECK_TV_WEBHOOK_SECRET=generate-a-long-random-string
#
# Private-workspace shared key. ops/start-local-workspace.ps1 (Next bound to
# 127.0.0.1) presents it in X-Signaldeck-Local so the daemon can serve licensed
# raw bars to THIS machine through the proxy. Honoured only from a loopback
# socket; EMPTY DISABLES the exception (fails closed). Never set it on a
# deployment whose web tier listens on a non-loopback interface.
# SIGNALDECK_LOCAL_PROXY_KEY=generate-a-long-random-string
#
# Rate limiting. Defaults: reads 10 req/s burst 30; writes rps/5 burst/6
# (floors of 1 and 2). Writes = every non-GET plus all of /api/ai/*.
# SIGNALDECK_RATE_RPS=10
# SIGNALDECK_RATE_BURST=30
#
# Assert you hold redistribution rights for stored price data. Default false.
# The flag RECORDS your assertion; it does not grant the right.
# SIGNALDECK_ALLOW_RAW_EXPORT=false

# ── MCP server (third-party AI agents) ────────────────────────────────────
# Never inherits an "open on loopback" default: exposing an interface built for
# someone else's agent is a compliance decision, made once, explicitly.
# SIGNALDECK_MCP_ENABLED=false
# HMAC key signing client keys. EMPTY = no key can verify, so nothing works.
# SIGNALDECK_MCP_SECRET=generate-with-openssl-rand-hex-32
# SIGNALDECK_MCP_AUDIT=<root>/signaldeck/logs/mcp_audit.jsonl
# SIGNALDECK_MCP_DAILY_CALLS=0
# SIGNALDECK_MCP_REVOKED=client-id-1,client-id-2

# ── Backups ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Offsite backup destination, read by ops/signaldeck-backup-offline.sh.
# Unset falls back to iCloud on macOS and %OneDrive%\SignalDeckBackups on
# Windows; with neither, the log says "offsite SKIPPED" and NOTHING is copied
# off this machine — which is exactly what it said every night from the
# Windows migration until 2026-08-07. Prefer an external volume: OneDrive
# survives losing the disk, a second physical drive survives more.
# Confirm logs/backup-offline.log says "offsite OK" before trusting it.
# SIGNALDECK_OFFSITE_DIR=D:\SignalDeckBackups
#
# S3 destination. Takes PRECEDENCE over SIGNALDECK_OFFSITE_DIR, and when it is
# set the DAEMON's own nightly offsite copy stands down entirely — the
# market-close script owns the upload, so exactly one process writes the
# backup_offsite_dir meta key. Two writers on that key is what once produced
# offsiteConfigured:false beside a lastOffsiteTs from minutes ago.
#
# Must be an s3:// URI; a bare bucket name or an https:// console URL is
# ignored with a warning rather than half-configured. Needs the aws CLI on PATH
# and working credentials — with neither, the run records a dq_event and does
# NOT stamp backup_last_offsite, so the staleness alarm keeps firing.
#
# Uploads the GZIPPED copy (~925 MB, vs 5.3 GB raw) every night, and the object
# is read BACK with head-object and size-checked before anything is recorded.
# Set a lifecycle rule on the bucket to expire old copies; nothing here prunes
# the remote side, and at this size unbounded retention gets expensive.
# SIGNALDECK_OFFSITE_S3=s3://signaldeck-backups/nightly

# ── Safety switches and provenance overrides ──────────────────────────────
# These were undocumented until 2026-08-09, which is the worst possible state
# for a switch that disables a safety check: an escape hatch nobody has written
# down is an escape hatch nobody audits. None of them belong in a normal
# .env — they are listed so that finding one SET is recognisable as a decision
# somebody made, rather than as ambient configuration.
#
# HALT — stop trading/publishing work without stopping the daemon.
#   SIGNALDECK_KILL_SWITCH overrides where the halt file lives (default
#   ops/HALT, relative to the daemon's working directory). The file's contents
#   become the audited reason, so write a sentence in it.
#   SIGNALDECK_HALT halts with no file at all, for a container or CI run with
#   no writable path of its own. Any value that is not an explicit off is a
#   halt: an ambiguous value is treated as an operator who meant something.
# SIGNALDECK_KILL_SWITCH=ops/HALT
# SIGNALDECK_HALT=1
#
# PROVENANCE OVERRIDES — each one disables a gate that exists so a published
# row can be traced to the exact commit that produced it. Rows written under
# any of these are UNGRADABLE, and nothing downstream can tell afterwards.
#   SIGNALDECK_ALLOW_DIRTY_BUILD=1  daemon starts from a dirty or unknown tree
#     instead of refusing. Development only; the daemon logs a warning naming
#     the rows it is about to make ungradable.
#   SIGNALDECK_ALLOW_HISTORY_REWRITE=1  ops/githooks/pre-rebase skips the
#     ledger check and permits a rebase/amend over anchored history.
#   SIGNALDECK_ALLOW_DIRECT_MAIN_PUSH=1  ops/githooks/pre-push permits pushing
#     straight to main, bypassing review.
#   SIGNALDECK_LEDGER_ANCHOR_DISABLE=1  stops publishing ledger anchors, so the
#     tamper-evidence chain simply stops extending. `intact:true` on
#     /api/ledger/verify then means only "internally consistent".
# SIGNALDECK_ALLOW_DIRTY_BUILD=1
# SIGNALDECK_ALLOW_HISTORY_REWRITE=1
# SIGNALDECK_ALLOW_DIRECT_MAIN_PUSH=1
# SIGNALDECK_LEDGER_ANCHOR_DISABLE=1
#
# STALE BINARY — ops/daemon-guard.ps1 warns and starts anyway when
# bin\signaldeckd.exe is behind HEAD (it once sat 22 commits behind across two
# restarts). Set to `refuse` to choose an outage over stale data instead.
# SIGNALDECK_ON_STALE_BINARY=refuse
